window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"56d19ac77c64bfd826f6ca25a066091d283e469df4e270a7ee6ba243a797fea9":{"markdown":"# A dual weight from compact coefficient graphs\n\n*New expression: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.*\n\nLet \\(G\\) be an arbitrary locally compact Hausdorff group. Fix left Haar measure with \\(\\mu(Es)=\\delta(s)\\mu(E)\\). Let \\(\\alpha\\) be a point-ultraweak continuous action by normal automorphisms of an arbitrary von Neumann algebra \\(M\\), and let \\(\\varphi\\) be faithful normal semifinite. There is no separability, countability, unimodularity, invariant-weight or finite-state hypothesis. Scalar products are linear in the first variable. The zero algebra has the zero Hilbert space and zero weight; below we discuss the nonzero case.\n\nThe existing arbitrary-weight proofs GW/NF/WR give the entire finite ideal \\(N=N_\\varphi\\), its faithful normal GNS representation on \\(H=H_\\varphi\\), and its full finite-star algebra. We identify \\(M\\) with that representation. The exact earlier inputs are [GW1–5](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1), [NF5](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5), [WR1–5](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.1), [BC1–5](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.1), [CI1–3](OA-FLOW-CI.md#oa-flow.ci.1), the pure completion proof [WH04 Sections2–4](OA-FLOW-WH04.md#oa-flow.wh04.2), [WF1–6](OA-FLOW-WF.md#oa-flow.wf.1), [MW1–4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.1), [SF](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sf0), and the actual [AT](OA-FLOW-AT.md#oa-flow.at.1), [NR](OA-FLOW-NR.md#oa-flow.nr.1), [CCM4](OA-FLOW-CAI.md#ccm-4), [HR](OA-FLOW-HR.md#hr-03) and [L24](OA-FLOW-L24.md#oa-flow.grp.haarconventions) integration and topology proofs. The scalar Borel and regularity arguments are [SC3–5](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-03) and [HR3](OA-FLOW-HR.md#hr-03); the compact finite partitions are [topology H0](OA-FLOW-TOPOLOGY.md#oa-flow.hr.topology). These are written programme proofs. Historical source context is Haagerup, *Dual weights I*, printed pp.102–114. His joint varying-weight cocycle continuity input is replaced here by compact graph approximation and is subsequently derived from the constructed modular action.\n\n<a id=\"gdw-0\"><\/a>\n\n## GDW0. What is constructed\n\nWe construct a left Hilbert algebra on \\(K=L^2(G,H)\\), prove its whole closed involution and adjoint domains, complete it without changing that involution, and obtain a faithful normal semifinite weight \\(\\widehat\\varphi\\) on the normal crossed product\n\n<a id=\"equation-gdw1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nR=\\{\\pi(M),\\lambda(G)\\}'',\\qquad\n(\\pi(a)\\xi)(t)=\\alpha_{t^{-1}}(a)\\xi(t),\\quad\n(\\lambda_s\\xi)(t)=\\xi(s^{-1}t).\n\\tag{GDW1}\n\\]\nThe theorem is proved in GDW1–8; this forward statement is not an earlier proved premise. Averaging operator-valued weights and comparison of dual weights for two different input weights are further statements, not conclusions inferred from this construction.\n\n<a id=\"gdw-1\"><\/a>","html":"<h1>A dual weight from compact coefficient graphs<\/h1>\n<p><em>New expression: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> be an arbitrary locally compact Hausdorff group. Fix left Haar measure with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu(Es)=\\delta(s)\\mu(E)\\)\">\\(\\mu(Es)=\\delta(s)\\mu(E)\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> be a point-ultraweak continuous action by normal automorphisms of an arbitrary von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> be faithful normal semifinite. There is no separability, countability, unimodularity, invariant-weight or finite-state hypothesis. Scalar products are linear in the first variable. The zero algebra has the zero Hilbert space and zero weight; below we discuss the nonzero case.<\/p>\n<p>The existing arbitrary-weight proofs GW/NF/WR give the entire finite ideal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=N_\\varphi\\)\">\\(N=N_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, its faithful normal GNS representation on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=H_\\varphi\\)\">\\(H=H_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, and its full finite-star algebra. We identify <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> with that representation. The exact earlier inputs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW1–5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">NF5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.1\">WR1–5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.1\">BC1–5<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CI.html#oa-flow.ci.1\">CI1–3<\/a>, the pure completion proof <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WH04.html#oa-flow.wh04.2\">WH04 Sections2–4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WF.html#oa-flow.wf.1\">WF1–6<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.1\">MW1–4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sf0\">SF<\/a>, and the actual <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-AT.html#oa-flow.at.1\">AT<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NR.html#oa-flow.nr.1\">NR<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAI.html#ccm-4\">CCM4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HR.html#hr-03\">HR<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L24.html#oa-flow.grp.haarconventions\">L24<\/a> integration and topology proofs. The scalar Borel and regularity arguments are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-03\">SC3–5<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HR.html#hr-03\">HR3<\/a>; the compact finite partitions are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TOPOLOGY.html#oa-flow.hr.topology\">topology H0<\/a>. These are written programme proofs. Historical source context is Haagerup, <em>Dual weights I<\/em>, printed pp.102–114. His joint varying-weight cocycle continuity input is replaced here by compact graph approximation and is subsequently derived from the constructed modular action.<\/p>\n<h2>GDW0. What is constructed<\/h2>\n<p>We construct a left Hilbert algebra on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=L^2(G,H)\\)\">\\(K=L^2(G,H)\\)<\/span>, prove its whole closed involution and adjoint domains, complete it without changing that involution, and obtain a faithful normal semifinite weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\widehat\\varphi\\)<\/span> on the normal crossed product<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nR=\\{\\pi(M),\\lambda(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad\n(\\pi(a)\\xi)(t)=\\alpha_{t^{-1}}(a)\\xi(t),\\quad\n(\\lambda_s\\xi)(t)=\\xi(s^{-1}t).\n\\tag{GDW1}\n\\]\">\\[\nR=\\{\\pi(M),\\lambda(G)\\}&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad\n(\\pi(a)\\xi)(t)=\\alpha_{t^{-1}}(a)\\xi(t),\\quad\n(\\lambda_s\\xi)(t)=\\xi(s^{-1}t).\n\\tag{GDW1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe theorem is proved in GDW1–8; this forward statement is not an earlier proved premise. Averaging operator-valued weights and comparison of dual weights for two different input weights are further statements, not conclusions inferred from this construction.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-GDW.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":2708,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"56ead1009f9a212a8106663b39257fc653344b145b278765884379a2102b99e6":{"markdown":"## Fixed-center test and a doubled translation system\n\n![Fixed center, exact shear and two surviving components](../assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion.png)\n\nThe upper panels record the proof for every nonzero von Neumann algebra and every locally compact Hausdorff abelian group in [the setting](OA-FLOW-L116.md#fc-setting). For \\(z\\in Z(N)^\\theta\\), the full dual-fixed-algebra theorem writes \\(z=i(c)\\). Faithfulness of \\(i\\) turns \\(i([c,a])=0\\) into \\(c\\in Z(M)\\); covariance turns \\(i(\\alpha_s(c))=i(c)\\) into \\(\\alpha_s(c)=c\\). The converse checks both generating families and their strong closure. This proves exactly \\(Z(N)^\\theta=i(Z(M)^\\alpha)\\), [F5 and FC1–FC2](OA-FLOW-L116.md#fc-direct). Full Connes spectrum makes the entire center dual-fixed by the complete earlier kernel proof. Central ergodicity then gives \\(Z(N)=\\mathbb C1\\), [F16–F17](OA-FLOW-L116.md#fc-factor). The diagram does not replace either proof or its separately bound L115 input.\n\nThe lower panels specialize only the drawing to \\(G=\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z\\) with counting Haar measure. Thus \\(K=L^2(G)=\\mathbb C^3\\), the regular tensor space has dimension \\(9\\), and the shear \\((r,t)\\mapsto(u,v)=(r+t,r)\\) is a permutation of all nine coordinate vectors. Its inverse is \\((r,t)=(v,u-v)\\), with all coordinates reduced modulo three. The color identifies the retained coordinate \\(v=r\\); the curved arrows and their lengths are layout choices, not a metric on the group. The actual unitary is \\([S\\xi](u,v)=\\xi(v,u-v)\\), and its [full unrestricted proof](OA-FLOW-L116.md#fc-translation) first uses compact continuous Radon-product functions before extending to the whole Hilbert space.\n\nIn the active coordinate \\(u\\), \\(Si_0(f)S^*=M_f\\otimes1\\) and \\(S\\lambda_sS^*=L_s\\otimes1\\), where \\((L_s\\xi)_r=\\xi_{r-s}\\). The second tensor coordinate is multiplicity, so the represented single-system crossing is \\(B(K)\\otimes1\\), normally isomorphic to \\(B(K)\\) by unit-vector compression. For \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\) and \\(Q_k=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\omega^{-k},\\omega^{-2k})\\), the exact character convention gives \\(Q_kL_sQ_k^*=\\omega^{-ks}L_s\\). The renderer checks all nine pairs; the floating matrix check is an illustration check, while the scalar identity proves the sign exactly.\n\nThe doubled coefficient system has \\(P_1=I_3\\oplus0\\), \\(P_2=0\\oplus I_3\\), with \\(P_1P_2=0\\) and \\(P_1+P_2=I_6\\). Its algebra is \\(M_3\\oplus M_3\\), not \\(M_6\\): there are no off-diagonal algebra blocks, and the isolated translations and diagonal coordinate projections produce every matrix unit inside each block. The dual action is \\(\\theta_k=\\operatorname{Ad}(Q_k\\oplus Q_k)\\), so it fixes \\(\\mathbb CP_1\\oplus\\mathbb CP_2\\) pointwise. This is the exact finite model of [F20–F22](OA-FLOW-L116.md#fc-two-components): \\(\\Gamma(\\alpha)=\\widehat G\\) while the crossing has a two-dimensional center and is not a factor. The body proves the same counterexample for arbitrary LCA \\(G\\); finite coordinates illustrate that conclusion without imposing a countability hypothesis on it.\n\nHistorical source: Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*, Corollary XI.2.8, printed336](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4). The direct test, arbitrary-basis tensor calculation and translation shear are fully written in the linked programme proof. Native image: \\(3200\\times2200\\). Editable [SVG](../assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion.svg), [exact coordinate and matrix data](../assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion-data.json), and [renderer](../assets/factor-criterion/render_figure.py). Original figure, caption, data and renderer: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; DejaVu has its retained [font terms](../assets/factor-criterion/assets/FONT-LICENSE.txt).","html":"<h2>Fixed-center test and a doubled translation system<\/h2>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion.png\" alt=\"Fixed center, exact shear and two surviving components\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The upper panels record the proof for every nonzero von Neumann algebra and every locally compact Hausdorff abelian group in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L116.html#fc-setting\">the setting<\/a>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z(N)^\\theta\\)\">\\(z\\in Z(N)^\\theta\\)<\/span>, the full dual-fixed-algebra theorem writes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=i(c)\\)\">\\(z=i(c)\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span> turns <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i([c,a])=0\\)\">\\(i([c,a])=0\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in Z(M)\\)\">\\(c\\in Z(M)\\)<\/span>; covariance turns <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i(\\alpha_s(c))=i(c)\\)\">\\(i(\\alpha_s(c))=i(c)\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_s(c)=c\\)\">\\(\\alpha_s(c)=c\\)<\/span>. The converse checks both generating families and their strong closure. This proves exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)^\\theta=i(Z(M)^\\alpha)\\)\">\\(Z(N)^\\theta=i(Z(M)^\\alpha)\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L116.html#fc-direct\">F5 and FC1–FC2<\/a>. Full Connes spectrum makes the entire center dual-fixed by the complete earlier kernel proof. Central ergodicity then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z(N)=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L116.html#fc-factor\">F16–F17<\/a>. The diagram does not replace either proof or its separately bound L115 input.<\/p>\n<p>The lower panels specialize only the drawing to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(G=\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> with counting Haar measure. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=L^2(G)=\\mathbb C^3\\)\">\\(K=L^2(G)=\\mathbb C^3\\)<\/span>, the regular tensor space has dimension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(9\\)\">\\(9\\)<\/span>, and the shear <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((r,t)\\mapsto(u,v)=(r+t,r)\\)\">\\((r,t)\\mapsto(u,v)=(r+t,r)\\)<\/span> is a permutation of all nine coordinate vectors. Its inverse is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((r,t)=(v,u-v)\\)\">\\((r,t)=(v,u-v)\\)<\/span>, with all coordinates reduced modulo three. The color identifies the retained coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=r\\)\">\\(v=r\\)<\/span>; the curved arrows and their lengths are layout choices, not a metric on the group. The actual unitary is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([S\\xi](u,v)=\\xi(v,u-v)\\)\">\\([S\\xi](u,v)=\\xi(v,u-v)\\)<\/span>, and its <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L116.html#fc-translation\">full unrestricted proof<\/a> first uses compact continuous Radon-product functions before extending to the whole Hilbert space.<\/p>\n<p>In the active coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Si_0(f)S^*=M_f\\otimes1\\)\">\\(Si_0(f)S^*=M_f\\otimes1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\lambda_sS^*=L_s\\otimes1\\)\">\\(S\\lambda_sS^*=L_s\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((L_s\\xi)_r=\\xi_{r-s}\\)\">\\((L_s\\xi)_r=\\xi_{r-s}\\)<\/span>. The second tensor coordinate is multiplicity, so the represented single-system crossing is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(K)\\otimes1\\)\">\\(B(K)\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, normally isomorphic to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(K)\\)\">\\(B(K)\\)<\/span> by unit-vector compression. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\)\">\\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_k=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\omega^{-k},\\omega^{-2k})\\)\">\\(Q_k=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\omega^{-k},\\omega^{-2k})\\)<\/span>, the exact character convention gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_kL_sQ_k^*=\\omega^{-ks}L_s\\)\">\\(Q_kL_sQ_k^*=\\omega^{-ks}L_s\\)<\/span>. The renderer checks all nine pairs; the floating matrix check is an illustration check, while the scalar identity proves the sign exactly.<\/p>\n<p>The doubled coefficient system has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_1=I_3\\oplus0\\)\">\\(P_1=I_3\\oplus0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_2=0\\oplus I_3\\)\">\\(P_2=0\\oplus I_3\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_1P_2=0\\)\">\\(P_1P_2=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_1+P_2=I_6\\)\">\\(P_1+P_2=I_6\\)<\/span>. Its algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_3\\oplus M_3\\)\">\\(M_3\\oplus M_3\\)<\/span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_6\\)\">\\(M_6\\)<\/span>: there are no off-diagonal algebra blocks, and the isolated translations and diagonal coordinate projections produce every matrix unit inside each block. The dual action is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_k=\\operatorname{Ad}(Q_k\\oplus Q_k)\\)\">\\(\\theta_k=\\operatorname{Ad}(Q_k\\oplus Q_k)\\)<\/span>, so it fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb CP_1\\oplus\\mathbb CP_2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb CP_1\\oplus\\mathbb CP_2\\)<\/span> pointwise. This is the exact finite model of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L116.html#fc-two-components\">F20–F22<\/a>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma(\\alpha)=\\widehat G\\)\">\\(\\Gamma(\\alpha)=\\widehat G\\)<\/span> while the crossing has a two-dimensional center and is not a factor. The body proves the same counterexample for arbitrary LCA <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>; finite coordinates illustrate that conclusion without imposing a countability hypothesis on it.<\/p>\n<p>Historical source: Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, Corollary XI.2.8, printed336<\/a>. The direct test, arbitrary-basis tensor calculation and translation shear are fully written in the linked programme proof. Native image: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(3200\\times2200\\)\">\\(3200\\times2200\\)<\/span>. Editable <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion.svg\">SVG<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/assets/factor-criterion-data.json\">exact coordinate and matrix data<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/render_figure.py\">renderer<\/a>. Original figure, caption, data and renderer: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; DejaVu has its retained <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/factor-criterion/assets/FONT-LICENSE.txt\">font terms<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L116.md","start_offset":24267,"end_offset":28019,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"56688cd89ad8f1cb1a353d20459f7b0101d94364f7032332a252463073105ad9":{"markdown":"<a id=\"open-corner-unitary\"><\/a>\n## The regular corner, with the Haar factor included\n\nChoose a faithful normal representation \\(N\\subseteq B(K)\\). Represent \\(M=\\prod_Y N\\) diagonally on \\(\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_YK\\). The regular representation of \\(P\\) acts on \\(L^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\), with\n\\[\n [\\pi(a)\\xi](g,y)\n   =\\beta_{c(g,y)^{-1}}(a_{gy})\\xi(g,y),\\qquad\n [u_r\\xi](g,y)=\\xi(r^{-1}g,y).\n \\tag{OS15}\n\\]\nThese formulas are first checked on finite-coordinate elementary vectors; the normal regular construction extends them to the entire Hilbert space. The vector-integration and tensor identification in [Haar conventions and Hilbert-valued integration](OA-FLOW-L24.md#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration) are valid for arbitrary \\(K\\) and \\(G\\).\n\nThe projection \\(p\\) retains exactly those pairs \\((g,y)\\) for which \\(gy=o\\). For fixed \\(y\\), this is the open set \\(Ht_y^{-1}\\). Write \\(g=ht_y^{-1}\\). Then \\(c(g,y)=h\\), since \\(gy=o\\) and \\(t_o=e\\). Define\n\\[\n T:pL^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\longrightarrow\n       \\bigoplus_{y\\in Y}L^2(H;K),\\qquad\n (T\\xi)_y(h)=\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1/2}\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y).\n \\tag{OS16}\n\\]\nThe scalar is forced by right Haar transport. Since \\(m_H=m_G|_H\\),\n\\[\n \\int_{Ht_y^{-1}}\\|\\xi(g,y)\\|^2\\,dm_G(g)\n =\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1}\n       \\int_H\\|\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y)\\|^2\\,dm_H(h).\n \\tag{OS17}\n\\]\nThus \\(T\\) preserves norms on every summand. The inverse multiplies by\n\\(\\Delta_G(t_y)^{1/2}\\) and transports \\(h\\) to \\(ht_y^{-1}\\). Both transformations preserve completed null sets because their measures differ by a positive scalar. They give inverse isometries on the full summands, and hence a unitary on their arbitrary Hilbert direct sum.\n\nFor \\(a\\in N\\), \\(r,h\\in H\\), (OS15) gives on every \\(y\\)-summand\n\\[\n [Tq(a)T^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\beta_{h^{-1}}(a)\\eta_y(h),\\qquad\n [Tw_rT^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\eta_y(r^{-1}h).\n \\tag{OS18}\n\\]\nIn the second formula, \\(r^{-1}ht_y^{-1}\\) stays in the same sector and the two constant Haar factors cancel. These are precisely the regular coefficient and left regular group operators for \\(N\\rtimes_\\beta H\\), repeated identically on every summand. Equations (OS14) and (OS18) therefore prove\n\\[\n TQT^*=(N\\rtimes_\\beta H)\\otimes1_{\\ell^2Y},\n \\qquad Q\\cong N\\rtimes_\\beta H.\n \\tag{OS19}\n\\]\nThe order of the displayed tensor factors uses the canonical direct-sum identification. Amplification is normal; compression to any one \\(y\\)-summand is its normal inverse. Its faithfulness is immediate from that compression. In particular we have proved the required regular topology of the corner, rather than inferring it from covariance alone.","html":"<h2>The regular corner, with the Haar factor included<\/h2>\n<p>Choose a faithful normal representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\subseteq B(K)\\)\">\\(N\\subseteq B(K)\\)<\/span>. Represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\prod_Y N\\)\">\\(M=\\prod_Y N\\)<\/span> diagonally on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_YK\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_YK\\)<\/span>. The regular representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> acts on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\)\">\\(L^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [\\pi(a)\\xi](g,y)\n   =\\beta_{c(g,y)^{-1}}(a_{gy})\\xi(g,y),\\qquad\n [u_r\\xi](g,y)=\\xi(r^{-1}g,y).\n \\tag{OS15}\n\\]\">\\[\n [\\pi(a)\\xi](g,y)\n   =\\beta_{c(g,y)^{-1}}(a_{gy})\\xi(g,y),\\qquad\n [u_r\\xi](g,y)=\\xi(r^{-1}g,y).\n \\tag{OS15}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese formulas are first checked on finite-coordinate elementary vectors; the normal regular construction extends them to the entire Hilbert space. The vector-integration and tensor identification in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L24.html#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration\">Haar conventions and Hilbert-valued integration<\/a> are valid for arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> retains exactly those pairs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((g,y)\\)\">\\((g,y)\\)<\/span> for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gy=o\\)\">\\(gy=o\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>, this is the open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Ht_y^{-1}\\)\">\\(Ht_y^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g=ht_y^{-1}\\)\">\\(g=ht_y^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c(g,y)=h\\)\">\\(c(g,y)=h\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gy=o\\)\">\\(gy=o\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_o=e\\)\">\\(t_o=e\\)<\/span>. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T:pL^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\longrightarrow\n       \\bigoplus_{y\\in Y}L^2(H;K),\\qquad\n (T\\xi)_y(h)=\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1/2}\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y).\n \\tag{OS16}\n\\]\">\\[\n T:pL^2(G;\\mathcal K)\\longrightarrow\n       \\bigoplus_{y\\in Y}L^2(H;K),\\qquad\n (T\\xi)_y(h)=\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1/2}\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y).\n \\tag{OS16}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe scalar is forced by right Haar transport. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m_H=m_G|_H\\)\">\\(m_H=m_G|_H\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{Ht_y^{-1}}\\|\\xi(g,y)\\|^2\\,dm_G(g)\n =\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1}\n       \\int_H\\|\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y)\\|^2\\,dm_H(h).\n \\tag{OS17}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{Ht_y^{-1}}\\|\\xi(g,y)\\|^2\\,dm_G(g)\n =\\Delta_G(t_y)^{-1}\n       \\int_H\\|\\xi(ht_y^{-1},y)\\|^2\\,dm_H(h).\n \\tag{OS17}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> preserves norms on every summand. The inverse multiplies by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_G(t_y)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(\\Delta_G(t_y)^{1/2}\\)<\/span> and transports <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ht_y^{-1}\\)\">\\(ht_y^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Both transformations preserve completed null sets because their measures differ by a positive scalar. They give inverse isometries on the full summands, and hence a unitary on their arbitrary Hilbert direct sum.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in N\\)\">\\(a\\in N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r,h\\in H\\)\">\\(r,h\\in H\\)<\/span>, (OS15) gives on every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>-summand\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [Tq(a)T^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\beta_{h^{-1}}(a)\\eta_y(h),\\qquad\n [Tw_rT^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\eta_y(r^{-1}h).\n \\tag{OS18}\n\\]\">\\[\n [Tq(a)T^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\beta_{h^{-1}}(a)\\eta_y(h),\\qquad\n [Tw_rT^*\\eta]_y(h)=\\eta_y(r^{-1}h).\n \\tag{OS18}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn the second formula, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1}ht_y^{-1}\\)\">\\(r^{-1}ht_y^{-1}\\)<\/span> stays in the same sector and the two constant Haar factors cancel. These are precisely the regular coefficient and left regular group operators for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\rtimes_\\beta H\\)\">\\(N\\rtimes_\\beta H\\)<\/span>, repeated identically on every summand. Equations (OS14) and (OS18) therefore prove\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n TQT^*=(N\\rtimes_\\beta H)\\otimes1_{\\ell^2Y},\n \\qquad Q\\cong N\\rtimes_\\beta H.\n \\tag{OS19}\n\\]\">\\[\n TQT^*=(N\\rtimes_\\beta H)\\otimes1_{\\ell^2Y},\n \\qquad Q\\cong N\\rtimes_\\beta H.\n \\tag{OS19}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe order of the displayed tensor factors uses the canonical direct-sum identification. Amplification is normal; compression to any one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>-summand is its normal inverse. Its faithfulness is immediate from that compression. In particular we have proved the required regular topology of the corner, rather than inferring it from covariance alone.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L55.md","start_offset":7775,"end_offset":10330,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"56ce8307e692c18c92e456300abb33d8776afb8c4c34fd5254970341db7289a6":{"markdown":"<a id=\"ext-square\"><\/a>\n## 5. Recovering every term of the square\n\nSuppose (EX13) holds. The following exactness statement is algebraic: it uses no closedness hypothesis on an abstract central kernel and no topology on the reconstructed group. Set\n\\[\n A_0=A^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad E_0=E_m^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad D=E_0/A_0.\n \\tag{EX16}\n\\]\nThe rows and columns of the following array are exact, with identity groups understood at both ends of each row and column:\n\\[\n \\begin{array}{ccccc}\n A_0&\\longrightarrow&A&\\xrightarrow{\\delta_A}&B\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&&\\big\\downarrow&&\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n E_0&\\longrightarrow&E_m&\\xrightarrow{\\delta_E}&Z\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&&\\big\\downarrow&&\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n D&\\longrightarrow&N&\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&H.\n \\end{array}\n \\tag{EX17}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.** For \\(x,y\\in E_m\\), centrality of \\(x^{-1}\\theta_t(x)\\) gives\n\\[\n (xy)^{-1}\\theta_t(xy)\n =y^{-1}\\delta_E(x)(t)\\theta_t(y)\n =\\delta_E(x)(t)\\delta_E(y)(t).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\delta_E\\) is a homomorphism, and its kernel is exactly \\(E_0\\). It is onto by (EX13). The corresponding assertions for \\(\\delta_A\\) follow from its definition. The left column is the definition of \\(D\\); the middle column is (EX5); the right column is the definition of \\(H\\). The induced \\(D\\to N\\) is injective because \\(E_0\\cap A=A_0\\).\n\nIf \\(n=q(x)\\) for \\(x\\in E_0\\), then \\(\\nu(n)=[\\delta_E(x)]=1\\). Conversely if \\(\\nu(n)=1\\), take any \\(x\\in q^{-1}(n)\\). Its displacement lies in \\(B\\), so choose \\(a\\in A\\) with \\(\\delta_A(a)=\\delta_E(x)\\). The element \\(a^{-1}x\\) is fixed and still has quotient \\(n\\). This proves exactness of the bottom row at \\(N\\). Its surjectivity follows from (EX13). Every square commutes by restriction or passage to quotients. \\(\\square\\)\n\nDefine the action on cocycles by\n\\[\n (gc)(t)=g(c(t))\\qquad(g\\in L).\n \\tag{EX18}\n\\]\nThe real subgroup is central in \\(L\\), so this preserves the cocycle identity and sends \\(\\delta_A(a)\\) to \\(\\delta_A(ga)\\). The same commutation gives \\(\\delta_E(gx)=g(\\delta_E(x))\\). Hence every arrow in (EX17) is equivariant. In particular \\(\\nu(gn)=g\\nu(n)\\), which can also be checked directly from the mixed factor identities.\n\nThe [next lesson's topology theorem](OA-FLOW-BRL.md#real-cocycle-topology) equips every term of this algebraic square with its precise subgroup or quotient topology. That argument proves the path-space and displacement assertions before using the Polish open-mapping theorem, and treats the possible non-Hausdorff quotient \\(H\\) explicitly.","html":"<h2>5. Recovering every term of the square<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose (EX13) holds. The following exactness statement is algebraic: it uses no closedness hypothesis on an abstract central kernel and no topology on the reconstructed group. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A_0=A^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad E_0=E_m^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad D=E_0/A_0.\n \\tag{EX16}\n\\]\">\\[\n A_0=A^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad E_0=E_m^{\\mathbb R},\\qquad D=E_0/A_0.\n \\tag{EX16}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe rows and columns of the following array are exact, with identity groups understood at both ends of each row and column:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{array}{ccccc}\n A_0&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;A&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\delta_A}&amp;B\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n E_0&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;E_m&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\delta_E}&amp;Z\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n D&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;N&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&amp;H.\n \\end{array}\n \\tag{EX17}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{array}{ccccc}\n A_0&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;A&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\delta_A}&amp;B\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n E_0&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;E_m&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\delta_E}&amp;Z\\\\\n \\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\big\\downarrow\\\\\n D&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;N&amp;\\xrightarrow{\\nu}&amp;H.\n \\end{array}\n \\tag{EX17}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,y\\in E_m\\)\">\\(x,y\\in E_m\\)<\/span>, centrality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^{-1}\\theta_t(x)\\)\">\\(x^{-1}\\theta_t(x)\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (xy)^{-1}\\theta_t(xy)\n =y^{-1}\\delta_E(x)(t)\\theta_t(y)\n =\\delta_E(x)(t)\\delta_E(y)(t).\n\\]\">\\[\n (xy)^{-1}\\theta_t(xy)\n =y^{-1}\\delta_E(x)(t)\\theta_t(y)\n =\\delta_E(x)(t)\\delta_E(y)(t).\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_E\\)\">\\(\\delta_E\\)<\/span> is a homomorphism, and its kernel is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\)<\/span>. It is onto by (EX13). The corresponding assertions for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A\\)\">\\(\\delta_A\\)<\/span> follow from its definition. The left column is the definition of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>; the middle column is (EX5); the right column is the definition of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>. The induced <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\to N\\)\">\\(D\\to N\\)<\/span> is injective because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\cap A=A_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\cap A=A_0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=q(x)\\)\">\\(n=q(x)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in E_0\\)\">\\(x\\in E_0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(n)=[\\delta_E(x)]=1\\)\">\\(\\nu(n)=[\\delta_E(x)]=1\\)<\/span>. Conversely if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(n)=1\\)\">\\(\\nu(n)=1\\)<\/span>, take any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in q^{-1}(n)\\)\">\\(x\\in q^{-1}(n)\\)<\/span>. Its displacement lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, so choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A(a)=\\delta_E(x)\\)\">\\(\\delta_A(a)=\\delta_E(x)\\)<\/span>. The element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^{-1}x\\)\">\\(a^{-1}x\\)<\/span> is fixed and still has quotient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. This proves exactness of the bottom row at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Its surjectivity follows from (EX13). Every square commutes by restriction or passage to quotients. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Define the action on cocycles by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (gc)(t)=g(c(t))\\qquad(g\\in L).\n \\tag{EX18}\n\\]\">\\[\n (gc)(t)=g(c(t))\\qquad(g\\in L).\n \\tag{EX18}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe real subgroup is central in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, so this preserves the cocycle identity and sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A(a)\\)\">\\(\\delta_A(a)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A(ga)\\)\">\\(\\delta_A(ga)\\)<\/span>. The same commutation gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_E(gx)=g(\\delta_E(x))\\)\">\\(\\delta_E(gx)=g(\\delta_E(x))\\)<\/span>. Hence every arrow in (EX17) is equivariant. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(gn)=g\\nu(n)\\)\">\\(\\nu(gn)=g\\nu(n)\\)<\/span>, which can also be checked directly from the mixed factor identities.<\/p>\n<p>The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BRL.html#real-cocycle-topology\">next lesson's topology theorem<\/a> equips every term of this algebraic square with its precise subgroup or quotient topology. That argument proves the path-space and displacement assertions before using the Polish open-mapping theorem, and treats the possible non-Hausdorff quotient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> explicitly.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-BEX.md","start_offset":12669,"end_offset":15130,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"56039d14b9b4dc96221a320c74e4fc4dafdf59f231b1398a955dd78ef8d426fb":{"markdown":"<a id=\"nnr-foundations\"><\/a>\n## 8. Foundations, a weighted finite example and sources\n\nThe actual proof inputs are [CPP Section 1](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-compatible) and its gauge and action definitions; [FCT6–8](OA-FLOW-FCT.md#oa-flow.fct.6), with the preceding finite cancellation, central tiles, corner distortion and stability proofs FCT0–5; [TRP Section 1](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-setting), for normal trace GNS; [TRP Section 10](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-obstruction), for matrix units and \\(L^2\\)-density; and [TRP Section 8](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-bernoulli), for the positive quotient Bernoulli example. The Hilbert projections, trace Cauchy–Schwarz, finite-dimensional matrix algebra and scalar determinant identities use the earlier bounded operator foundations named there. Sections 3–5 supply compact probability subsequences, regular testing, coefficient measures, the fixed-space average, relative rigidity for this specific pair and the compact-eigenspace argument locally. There is no premise asserting that a rigid group embeds into, or is excluded from, an AFD factor. The negative proof needs only CPP's algebraic calculations and countable implementer choices; the general Borel-section and Polish-factor theorem is not needed.\n\nThe retained measure foundations are Lebesgue probability on \\([0,1]\\), finite product integration, Borel pushforward, continuity from below, dominated convergence and finite sums and integrals. The compact probability and regular-test arguments are proved in Section 3. This negative proof does not require an amenable-to-hyperfinite theorem or its measured-relation machinery. Those enter only the separately proved positive amenable boundary through ARP.\n\n**Weighted two-block example.** Inside \\(M_{18}\\), take\n\\[\n F=(M_2\\otimes I_3)\\oplus(M_3\\otimes I_4),\n \\qquad \\tau=\\tfrac1{18}\\operatorname{Tr}.\n\\]\nThe block traces are \\(t_1=6/18=1/3\\), \\(t_2=12/18=2/3\\). The Hilbert space \\(L^2(M_{18},\\tau)\\) has dimension \\(324\\), and \\(F\\) has dimension \\(4+9=13\\). With \\(\\operatorname{rank}P_1=4\\) and \\(\\operatorname{rank}P_2=9\\), formula (NNR10) becomes\n\\[\n D_F=\\sqrt{\\tfrac1{12}}P_1+\\sqrt{\\tfrac2{27}}P_2,\n \\qquad \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\tfrac4{12}+\\tfrac{18}{27}=1.\n\\]\nThe normalized unweighted projection \\((P_1+P_2)/\\sqrt{13}\\) also has Hilbert–Schmidt norm one, but its test at the first block unit gives \\(4/13\\), whereas the required value is \\(\\tau(p_1)=1/3\\). Thus normalization alone does not establish (NNR11). This is a concrete example of the weights, not a restriction on the approximating algebras.\n\nThe classical relative-rigidity background is Yves Cornulier, [*On Kazhdan's Property T*](https://www.normalesup.org/~cornulier/kaz.pdf), Theorem 3.2, printed p.22, and Emmanuel Breuillard, [*Exercises for the PCMI Summer School*](https://www.math.utah.edu/pcmi12/lecture_notes/breuillard-problems1.pdf), Section III, printed pp.3–4. Breuillard credits Burger's argument. These sources locate the human theorem and strategy. Here the finite Fejér densities, weighted block operator and every relative-rigidity deduction have been supplied in full.\n\nA related nonrealization result is Sergio Girón Pacheco, [*Projective representations on operator algebras*](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.02924v1), Theorem 3.1, printed p.12. Its symplectic-lattice and Deligne argument is not a premise of this semidirect-product proof. No novelty priority is claimed. The motivating exercise is Takesaki's [*Theory of Operator Algebras III*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10453-8), XVII.3 Exercise 5, printed pp.293–294, interpreted with the correction of Exercise 1(c) on p.292 established above.","html":"<h2>8. Foundations, a weighted finite example and sources<\/h2>\n<p>The actual proof inputs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-compatible\">CPP Section 1<\/a> and its gauge and action definitions; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FCT.html#oa-flow.fct.6\">FCT6–8<\/a>, with the preceding finite cancellation, central tiles, corner distortion and stability proofs FCT0–5; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-setting\">TRP Section 1<\/a>, for normal trace GNS; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-obstruction\">TRP Section 10<\/a>, for matrix units and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span>-density; and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-bernoulli\">TRP Section 8<\/a>, for the positive quotient Bernoulli example. The Hilbert projections, trace Cauchy–Schwarz, finite-dimensional matrix algebra and scalar determinant identities use the earlier bounded operator foundations named there. Sections 3–5 supply compact probability subsequences, regular testing, coefficient measures, the fixed-space average, relative rigidity for this specific pair and the compact-eigenspace argument locally. There is no premise asserting that a rigid group embeds into, or is excluded from, an AFD factor. The negative proof needs only CPP's algebraic calculations and countable implementer choices; the general Borel-section and Polish-factor theorem is not needed.<\/p>\n<p>The retained measure foundations are Lebesgue probability on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1]\\)\">\\([0,1]\\)<\/span>, finite product integration, Borel pushforward, continuity from below, dominated convergence and finite sums and integrals. The compact probability and regular-test arguments are proved in Section 3. This negative proof does not require an amenable-to-hyperfinite theorem or its measured-relation machinery. Those enter only the separately proved positive amenable boundary through ARP.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Weighted two-block example.<\/strong> Inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_{18}\\)\">\\(M_{18}\\)<\/span>, take\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F=(M_2\\otimes I_3)\\oplus(M_3\\otimes I_4),\n \\qquad \\tau=\\tfrac1{18}\\operatorname{Tr}.\n\\]\">\\[\n F=(M_2\\otimes I_3)\\oplus(M_3\\otimes I_4),\n \\qquad \\tau=\\tfrac1{18}\\operatorname{Tr}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe block traces are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_1=6/18=1/3\\)\">\\(t_1=6/18=1/3\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_2=12/18=2/3\\)\">\\(t_2=12/18=2/3\\)<\/span>. The Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(M_{18},\\tau)\\)\">\\(L^2(M_{18},\\tau)\\)<\/span> has dimension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(324\\)\">\\(324\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> has dimension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(4+9=13\\)\">\\(4+9=13\\)<\/span>. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{rank}P_1=4\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{rank}P_1=4\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{rank}P_2=9\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{rank}P_2=9\\)<\/span>, formula (NNR10) becomes\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D_F=\\sqrt{\\tfrac1{12}}P_1+\\sqrt{\\tfrac2{27}}P_2,\n \\qquad \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\tfrac4{12}+\\tfrac{18}{27}=1.\n\\]\">\\[\n D_F=\\sqrt{\\tfrac1{12}}P_1+\\sqrt{\\tfrac2{27}}P_2,\n \\qquad \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\tfrac4{12}+\\tfrac{18}{27}=1.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe normalized unweighted projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((P_1+P_2)/\\sqrt{13}\\)\">\\((P_1+P_2)/\\sqrt{13}\\)<\/span> also has Hilbert–Schmidt norm one, but its test at the first block unit gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(4/13\\)\">\\(4/13\\)<\/span>, whereas the required value is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau(p_1)=1/3\\)\">\\(\\tau(p_1)=1/3\\)<\/span>. Thus normalization alone does not establish (NNR11). This is a concrete example of the weights, not a restriction on the approximating algebras.<\/p>\n<p>The classical relative-rigidity background is Yves Cornulier, <a href=\"https://www.normalesup.org/~cornulier/kaz.pdf\"><em>On Kazhdan's Property T<\/em><\/a>, Theorem 3.2, printed p.22, and Emmanuel Breuillard, <a href=\"https://www.math.utah.edu/pcmi12/lecture_notes/breuillard-problems1.pdf\"><em>Exercises for the PCMI Summer School<\/em><\/a>, Section III, printed pp.3–4. Breuillard credits Burger's argument. These sources locate the human theorem and strategy. Here the finite Fejér densities, weighted block operator and every relative-rigidity deduction have been supplied in full.<\/p>\n<p>A related nonrealization result is Sergio Girón Pacheco, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.02924v1\"><em>Projective representations on operator algebras<\/em><\/a>, Theorem 3.1, printed p.12. Its symplectic-lattice and Deligne argument is not a premise of this semidirect-product proof. No novelty priority is claimed. The motivating exercise is Takesaki's <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10453-8\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras III<\/em><\/a>, XVII.3 Exercise 5, printed pp.293–294, interpreted with the correction of Exercise 1(c) on p.292 established above.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-NNR.md","start_offset":31626,"end_offset":35255,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"5606a818b6ff188649c74b07800a6b016355b73fed7d463b56df69d33ff22f22":{"markdown":"<a id=\"eor-7\"><\/a>\n\n## An uncountable example and the proof mechanism\n\nLet \\(G\\) be any uncountable discrete group, with counting Haar measure, acting on \\(M=\\ell^\\infty(G)\\) by left translation. This action is point-ultraweakly continuous because the group is discrete. It is ergodic: an invariant function has the same value at every point. Every nonnegative finitely supported function has the constant orbit average \\(\\sum_{s\\in G}f(s)\\); these functions are ultraweakly dense, since a predual element of \\(\\ell^1(G)\\) has at most countable support and its tails tend to zero. Thus the system is integrable, with \\(H=\\{e\\}\\).\n\nThe algebra has no faithful normal state. Every such state would be a positive summable family, supported on at most countably many points, and would vanish on a nonzero point projection outside that support. Its predual is nonseparable: the unit point masses form an uncountable family at pairwise distance two. This example shows why the proof constructs finite normal orbit functionals instead of assuming a global faithful normal state.\n\n![A finite orbit functional forces one proper orbit, then extends multiplication to the whole normal algebra.](../assets/ergodic-orbit/ergodic-orbit.svg)\n\nThe upper chain uses (EO13)–(EO18). The lower chain uses (EO16)–(EO26). The ideal is precisely the continuous functions vanishing on the closed orbit; the normal functional is nonzero and annihilates that ideal. Ergodicity forces its ultraweak closure to be zero. The final product over open sigma-compact pieces is an arbitrary bounded product, not a countable disintegration. [Editable vector figure](../assets/ergodic-orbit/ergodic-orbit.svg) and [reproduction source](../assets/ergodic-orbit/render_ergodic_orbit.py).\n\n**Exercise.** Which part of the proof prevents a finite orbit integral from being passed directly through an arbitrary spectrum character?\n\n**Solution.** Only compact norm integrals are evaluated at the character, giving the inequality (EO13). The full multiplication representation is extended normally later, through (EO17)–(EO21). No normality of the original character on \\(M\\) is assumed.\n\n**Exercise.** Does the factor \\(\\Delta(g)^{1/2}\\) in (EO22) contradict invariance of quotient Haar measure?\n\n**Solution.** That unitary uses \\(\\nu\\), obtained from the inverse orbit map. Equation (EO25) compares it with invariant quotient Haar \\(m\\); \\(\\nu(gE)=\\Delta(g)^{-1}\\nu(E)\\). After the density unitary to \\(L^2(m)\\), the same action is ordinary left translation without the scalar.\n\nThe classical classification is Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4), Lemma X.4.13, printed p.305. The proof here supplies the normal-functional and full-algebra steps explicitly and retains arbitrary locally compact groups. Original exposition, proof expression, figure and reproduction code are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.","html":"<h2>An uncountable example and the proof mechanism<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> be any uncountable discrete group, with counting Haar measure, acting on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\ell^\\infty(G)\\)\">\\(M=\\ell^\\infty(G)\\)<\/span> by left translation. This action is point-ultraweakly continuous because the group is discrete. It is ergodic: an invariant function has the same value at every point. Every nonnegative finitely supported function has the constant orbit average <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{s\\in G}f(s)\\)\">\\(\\sum_{s\\in G}f(s)\\)<\/span>; these functions are ultraweakly dense, since a predual element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^1(G)\\)\">\\(\\ell^1(G)\\)<\/span> has at most countable support and its tails tend to zero. Thus the system is integrable, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=\\{e\\}\\)\">\\(H=\\{e\\}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The algebra has no faithful normal state. Every such state would be a positive summable family, supported on at most countably many points, and would vanish on a nonzero point projection outside that support. Its predual is nonseparable: the unit point masses form an uncountable family at pairwise distance two. This example shows why the proof constructs finite normal orbit functionals instead of assuming a global faithful normal state.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/ergodic-orbit/ergodic-orbit.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/ergodic-orbit/ergodic-orbit.svg\" alt=\"A finite orbit functional forces one proper orbit, then extends multiplication to the whole normal algebra.\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The upper chain uses (EO13)–(EO18). The lower chain uses (EO16)–(EO26). The ideal is precisely the continuous functions vanishing on the closed orbit; the normal functional is nonzero and annihilates that ideal. Ergodicity forces its ultraweak closure to be zero. The final product over open sigma-compact pieces is an arbitrary bounded product, not a countable disintegration. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/ergodic-orbit/ergodic-orbit.svg\">Editable vector figure<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/ergodic-orbit/render_ergodic_orbit.py\">reproduction source<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Which part of the proof prevents a finite orbit integral from being passed directly through an arbitrary spectrum character?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> Only compact norm integrals are evaluated at the character, giving the inequality (EO13). The full multiplication representation is extended normally later, through (EO17)–(EO21). No normality of the original character on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is assumed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Does the factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta(g)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(\\Delta(g)^{1/2}\\)<\/span> in (EO22) contradict invariance of quotient Haar measure?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> That unitary uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span>, obtained from the inverse orbit map. Equation (EO25) compares it with invariant quotient Haar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(gE)=\\Delta(g)^{-1}\\nu(E)\\)\">\\(\\nu(gE)=\\Delta(g)^{-1}\\nu(E)\\)<\/span>. After the density unitary to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(m)\\)\">\\(L^2(m)\\)<\/span>, the same action is ordinary left translation without the scalar.<\/p>\n<p>The classical classification is Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em><\/a>, Lemma X.4.13, printed p.305. The proof here supplies the normal-functional and full-algebra steps explicitly and retains arbitrary locally compact groups. Original exposition, proof expression, figure and reproduction code are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-EOR.md","start_offset":21409,"end_offset":24332,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
